# IPv4 Communication: Unicast, Broadcast, and Multicast

## Overview
In IPv4 networking, communication happens in three main ways:
- Unicast → One-to-One
- Broadcast → One-to-All
- Multicast → One-to-Group

---

## 1. Unicast

Definition:
Communication between one sender and one receiver.

Key Points:
- Uses a unique IP address
- Most common type of communication
- Efficient and direct

Examples:
- Web browsing
- SSH, FTP

Diagram:
Sender ---> Receiver

---

## 2. Broadcast

Definition:
Communication from one sender to all devices in the network.

Key Points:
- Uses broadcast IP (e.g., 192.168.1.255)
- All devices receive the message
- Can cause network congestion

Examples:
- ARP request
- DHCP discovery

Diagram:
        ---> Device A
Sender ---> Device B
        ---> Device C

---

## 3. Multicast

Definition:
Communication from one sender to a selected group.

Key Points:
- Uses IP range 224.0.0.0 – 239.255.255.255
- Only interested devices receive data
- Efficient for group communication

Examples:
- Video streaming
- Online meetings

Diagram:
        ---> Group Member A
Sender ---> Group Member B
        ---> Group Member C

---

## Comparison Table

| Feature       | Unicast     | Broadcast     | Multicast      |
|--------------|------------|--------------|---------------|
| Type         | One-to-One | One-to-All   | One-to-Group  |
| Efficiency   | High       | Low          | High          |
| Traffic      | Low        | High         | Moderate      |
| Example      | Web        | ARP          | Streaming     |

---

## Network Segmentation

- Broadcast traffic stays within a subnet
- Routers block broadcast traffic
- Segmentation reduces congestion and improves performance

---

## Key Takeaways

- Unicast is the default communication
- Broadcast is useful but heavy on network
- Multicast is efficient for group communication